Plane 2: The Agentic Surface (GraphQL + MCP)
Why GraphQL? In the optical carrier and LLM attention space, payload size is strictly constrained:
A 17×17 chromatic optical carrier has a budget of 54–65 bytes.
A standard REST endpoint returns massive, bloated JSON objects with dozens of irrelevant fields.
GraphQL solves the optical chunking problem (the Rolldown analogy): An AI agent or edge reader queries only the exact scalar fields required:
graphql
query GetOpticalPermit($acctId: ID!) {
  account(id: $acctId) {
    generation
    merkleRoot
    permitSignature
  }
}
This trims a 2,000-byte record down to 38 tokens (48 bytes), allowing it to fit 100% inside a single optical frame or local prompt without chunking or network overhead.
Why Model Context Protocol (MCP)?
Exposes the engine as a native MCP Tool (vdb_read_record, vdb_commit_permit, vdb_verify_receipt) so desktop AI assistants (Claude, Gemini, or local models) can query and mutate local SQLite tables securely without granting them raw terminal or arbitrary file system access.
